    "knowledge_point": "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Decay Curve",
    "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of  $y=3{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}-1?$",
    "worked_solution": "We start with plotting the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941829/assets/4cdc9e4fa78513bca01fa10242ba6c3f12b31c262448a8edf350c76e84b188b8.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=3{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $3.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,3)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941829/assets/8e7d6c5eb931f55a9f94ac33382c0e1ec9e9e49f50b3ab538d9848d6a77be665.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=3{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}-1.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $1$ unit down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,3)$ to $(0,2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941829/assets/9d332fa8d3d8ef65daaf34bf906c5ca7cd7e2edaf562386ed4e0ebc4555b0a64.png)",
